@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:persistence] migration-snapshot-resource-agreement
Schema changes MUST reconcile the Ash resource definitions, new migrations and
resource snapshots. Every change to a deployed database MUST be a new migration
compatible with the release still serving while it runs. A table or column removed
from the Ash resources is dropped by a hand-written migration in a later push, after
both hosts converge (ADR-0159). Applied migration files MAY lose one-shot data
statements, purges or compatibility triggers whose effect is present in every
deployed database; such edits MUST NOT change the schema a fresh installation
produces, MUST retain required seed rows and MUST NOT be presented as an upgrade
path. Constraints and partial unique
indexes implementing admission, idempotency, active grants and writer fencing
MUST remain enforced by PostgreSQL, not solely application checks.

ADR-0147 explicitly authorizes spelling-only baseline reconciliation for fresh
installations. Those source-file edits MUST NOT be described as a deployed-schema
upgrade or an importer for existing databases or Lilly journals.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:deployment] explicit-release-migration
Production migrations MUST run through the release migration workflow before
dependent application versions start, either explicitly or in ADR-0036's locked
automatic pre-switch phase. Ordinary replica startup MUST NOT race to migrate
the shared database. Seed scripts MUST NOT insert working production credentials
or bypass protected host enrollment. A destructive migration MUST NOT be pushed
until overalls records the preceding push healthy.
